The core lies in prohibiting the compiler's implicit generation of a default constructor.  This can be done by:

1. **Deleting the default constructor:**
```c++
struct D1 {
  int x;
  D1(int val) : x(val) {} 
  D1() = delete;
};
```
2. **Declaring the constructor explicit (if it takes arguments):**  Note that this alone doesn't fully prevent all implicit conversions, so this example uses both explicit and delete methods.
```c++
struct D2 {
  int x;
  explicit D2(int val) : x(val) {} 
  D2() = delete; //This is the key part.
};
```

The compiler will reject any attempt to create an object of `D1` or `D2` without providing the constructor argument(s).
